  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency / more negative CRISPR dependency score in the exposed group; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency / less negative or higher score in the exposed group.\n\nThe batch_a robustness results support all four leading accepted associations. H62, USP7 dependency comparing TP53-wild-type with TP53-loss within screen_batch=batch_a, gave +0.789 (95% interval +0.749 to +0.828). This is far from zero and essentially matches the overall H6 discovery estimate. H64, SMARCA2 dependency with SMARCA4 loss in batch_a, gave +0.747 (0.709 to 0.785), slightly lower than the overall H7 estimate but still a very large association and far from zero. H66, WRN dependency with MSI-high in batch_a, gave +0.824 (0.766 to 0.882) despite only 37 MSI-high cells in batch_a; the interval is wide but very large and excludes zero. H68, NFE2L2 dependency with KEAP1 loss in batch_a in the reversed direction, gave +0.812 (0.765 to 0.858), indicating weaker NFE2L2 dependency / higher score in KEAP1-loss cells and supporting H13.\n\nThe high-screen-quality subset, defined by screen_roc_auc >= 0.95, also supports the same four effects. H70 USP7/TP53-wild-type was +0.804 (0.780 to 0.828). H71 SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss was +0.790 (0.765 to 0.816). H72 WRN/MSI-high was +0.795 (0.758 to 0.832). H73 NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss was +0.790 (0.763 to 0.816). These estimates are not attenuated toward zero and are consistent with the leading overall effects, making it less plausible that these are artifacts of low screen quality or noisy screens. Multiplicity remains a consideration for context tests, but the consistency across independent technical contexts and the magnitude relative to the dependency-score standard deviations are compelling within this dataset.\n\nThe USP7 aneuploidy-strata results further reduce concern that the TP53-wild-type/USP7 association is merely a byproduct of co-occurring aneuploidy. In high aneuploidy, H74 gave +0.801 (0.758 to 0.844); in low aneuploidy, H75 gave +0.816 (0.785 to 0.846). These intervals overlap and remain very large. H76 also strengthens WRN/MSI-high: in the stricter mutation_burden >= 10 stratum, the signed estimate was +0.768 (0.721 to 0.815), still extremely large, even when the comparator population is itself relatively high-mutation-burden. This supports H9 without establishing causality or external generalizability.\n\nThe one focused new broad hypothesis, H77 RB1 loss versus KIF18A dependency, is rejected: signed estimate +0.008 (interval -0.019 to +0.036). This is null and not scientifically meaningful given the KIF18A dependency-score scale. Like many other targeted same-gene or pathway-target hypotheses tested so far, it does not show a large measured predictor effect.\n\nGiven the new robustness evidence, I request independent validation of H9, the unrestricted WRN/MSI-high association. H9 is now supported by discovery, non-KY library robustness, adherent-culture robustness, batch_a, high screen quality, high mutation burden, and validation of the related H17 restricted claim, but it remains the only leading accepted main effect without direct independent validation. If validation confirms, H9 can be closed as a robust within-dataset association; if validation attenuates, the WRN claim should be narrowed to the specific contexts where it remains large. These findings remain associations within this supplied lung-cell-line dataset, not causal mechanisms or clinically validated biomarker claims.",
