  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive estimate means stronger dependency, that is a more negative CRISPR dependency score in the exposed group relative to comparator; for direction +1 claims, a positive estimate means weaker dependency, that is a higher or less negative score in the exposed group.\n\nDue evidence assessment. H9, the unrestricted WRN dependency comparison MSI-high versus not MSI-high, received independent validation R63: signed estimate +0.7896 with independent interval +0.7364 to +0.8429. Discovery R9 was +0.7899, interval +0.7604 to +0.8193. These are nearly identical and extremely large relative to the WRN dependency-score distribution, whose standard deviation is about 0.263. The validation interval remains far from zero even with the validation alpha accounting for multiple validation comparisons. I therefore accept H9 and close its investigation. The within-dataset claim is that MSI-high cells have stronger WRN dependency than non-MSI-high cells.\n\nH17, WRN dependency among mutation_burden >= 4, had validation deadline R39. Validation gave +0.8187, independent interval +0.7695 to +0.8680, closely aligned with discovery R17 +0.7869, interval +0.7567 to +0.8171. This confirms the high-mutation-burden-restricted WRN/MSI-high association and supports, but does not replace, H9 as the main WRN finding. H17 remains accepted and closed.\n\nH13, the reversed NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss claim, had validation deadline R38. Validation gave +0.8020, independent interval +0.7746 to +0.8294, while discovery R10 gave signed +0.7961, interval +0.7759 to +0.8163. Because direction +1 was registered to capture the reverse of the original mechanistic guess, a positive estimate means KEAP1-loss cells have weaker NFE2L2 dependency, i.e. higher or less negative dependency scores, than KEAP1-intact cells. This direction is counterintuitive but stable across discovery and validation and across several context checks. I accept H13 and close it.\n\nSynthesis of the current evidence: the strongest supported findings are four large measured predictor-outcome associations, now all directly independently validated within the supplied dataset or through the corresponding validated main claim. TP53-wild-type status is associated with stronger USP7 dependency (H6, validation R50 +0.811). SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H7, validation R13 +0.805). MSI-high status is associated with stronger WRN dependency (H9, validation R63 +0.790; H17 also validated +0.819 in the mutation_burden >= 4 population). KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, or higher NFE2L2 dependency score, than KEAP1-intact cells (H13, validation R38 +0.802; H10 in the opposite direction is rejected because the raw contrast points the other way).\n\nThese effects are scientifically meaningful within this dataset. The leading signed differences are roughly 0.75 to 0.82 dependency-score units, which is more than two standard deviations of the relevant dependency-score distributions and very far from zero. Interpreting them literally as percentages is awkward because dependency scores are often negative, but they are plainly larger than any plausible 10 percent clinical-significance threshold if judged on the outcome scale. The supporting context evidence is also unusually consistent: the four leading associations remain large in non-KY and KY CRISPR libraries, adherent culture, the largest screen batch, high screen-quality screens, different mutation-burden contexts, and both high- and low-aneuploidy strata for USP7.\n\nThe negative findings are an important part of the report. Direct same-gene or pathway-target hypotheses are broadly unsupported in this dataset: KRAS mutation did not predict KRAS dependency; BRAF V600E did not predict BRAF dependency; EGFR amplification did not predict EGFR dependency, including among KRAS/BRAF wild-type cells; ERBB2 amplification did not predict ERBB2 dependency; PIK3CA mutation did not predict STAT3 dependency; MYC amplification did not predict POLR2A dependency; high MYC expression did not support RPL11; TP53 loss did not predict MDM2 dependency; MSI-high did not predict DHX9 or KIF18A dependency; KRAS mutation did not show the predicted RIT1 pattern among BRAF wild-type cells; ERBB2 amplification did not predict TMED10 dependency; and RB1 loss did not predict KIF18A dependency. Interaction tests so far have not shown meaningful modification of the leading SMARCA2 or NFE2L2 associations by ARID1A loss or TP53-loss status. Formal batch and lineage interactions remain registered but unresolved; however, the main effects are already robust within batch_a, adenocarcinoma, library, culture, screen-quality, and mutation-burden contexts, and the direct validation of H9 closes the most important remaining gap.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty is now primarily interpretive and external, not statistical. These are associations within the supplied lung-cell-line-like dataset. They do not establish causality, mechanistic synthetic lethality, drug response, or clinical biomarker behavior. The KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 weaker-dependency direction is especially biologically counterintuitive and may reflect dataset-specific structure, co-alteration patterns, scoring behavior, or some unmeasured confounder. Likewise, USP7/TP53-wild-type could reflect genuine biology or co-occurring genomic/assay structure; the aneuploidy-strata results reduce one specific confounding concern but do not prove mechanism. Generalizability outside this dataset, lineage, screen library, culture setting, and batch structure remains untested.\n\nMost informative next analysis: because all leading main effects are validated, the next high-value work is to (1) formally test major technical modifiers of the validated effects, starting with library-specific heterogeneity as registered in the current proposal batch, and (2) probe additional biologically plausible predictor-outcome pairs, especially DNA-repair/mitotic-stress hypotheses such as BRCA2 loss for WRN, DHX9, KIF18A, and POLR2A, plus STK11 loss for KRAS dependency. If a new context or predictor estimate approaches the magnitude of the leading validated effects and is not already explained by the accepted associations, it would merit validation; otherwise, the current within-dataset conclusion is four robust dependency-score associations and a broad null result for many direct-target dependency predictions.",
  "research_notes": "Validated/accepted main associations: H6 USP7/TP53-wild-type stronger dependency, validated R50; H7 SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency, validated R13; H9 WRN/MSI-high stronger dependency unrestricted, discovery R9 and validation R63, now accept/closed; H13 NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss weaker dependency/higher score, discovery R10 signed +0.796 and validation R38 +0.802, accept/closed; H17 WRN/MSI-high in mutation_burden>=4, validated R39, accept/closed.\n\nContext robustness accepted across library, culture, batch_a, high screen quality, mutation burden, aneuploidy, adenocarcinoma, and other accepted context claims. Individual context estimates are accepted as within-dataset robustness support; direct validation remains focused on leading main effects.\n\nKey negatives/rejections: direct-target hypotheses mostly null, including KRAS/KRAS, BRAF/BRAF, EGFR/EGFR, ERBB2/ERBB2, PIK3CA/STAT3, MYC/POLR2A, high-MYC/RPL11, TP53/MDM2, MSI/DHX9, MSI/KIF18A, KRAS/RIT1, ERBB2/TMED10, RB1/KIF18A, MYC/MDM2 among TP53-WT. Interaction tests: no meaningful ARID1A modification of SMARCA2/SMARCA4, no TP53 modification of SMARCA2, no TP53 modification of NFE2L2/KEAP1. H10 opposite NFE2L2 direction rejected.\n\nCurrent interpretation: four large within-dataset associations, roughly +0.75 to +0.82 signed dependency-score units, >2 SD; robust but not causal or externally generalizable. KEAP1/NFE2L2 reversed direction remains stable but mechanistically unresolved.\n\nNext planned analyses: proposed library-interaction heterogeneity tests for USP7/TP53, SMARCA2/SMARCA4, WRN/MSI, and NFE2L2/KEAP1; plus focused new predictor-outcome tests of BRCA2 loss for WRN, DHX9, KIF18A, POLR2A, and STK11 loss for KRAS dependency. If any new large effect appears, request validation. Deferred formal batch/lineage interactions can be analyzed if needed, but main effects already robust in those contexts and H9 is now validated."
