high/TP53-loss cell is small, with only 24 exposed observations, so the interval is wide, but the effect remains far from zero. H90, the reversed NFE2L2/KEAP1-loss effect restricted to STK11-loss cells, gave +0.775 with interval +0.727 to +0.823, again supporting the counterintuitive direction that KEAP1-loss cells have weaker NFE2L2 dependency. These context checks make it less plausible that the leading associations are carried entirely by one co-occurring alteration context, but they remain within-dataset associations, not causal claims. I accept and close H87-H90.\n\nThe one genuinely new positive finding is H94: PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency, signed estimate +0.267 with interval +0.215 to +0.319, in 266 versus 1734 cell lines. This is not in the huge +0.75 to +0.85 range of the leading four effects, but it is still substantial on the dependency-score scale: the STAT3 dependency-score standard deviation is about 0.246, so this difference is roughly one standard deviation and is far beyond the null band seen in other predictor tests. The interval excludes zero and the sample sizes are adequate. Given the large number of comparisons tested, however, I cannot yet regard it as equally secure as the validated main effects. Its magnitude is scientifically meaningful, but its status should be tested independently. Therefore I accept H94 as a promising within-dataset association, keep its investigation active, and request independent validation of H94.\n\nThe main alternative explanations for H94 are residual confounding by correlated genomic or expression features, multiplicity across many null predictor-outcome tests, and dataset-specific biological structure. PTEN loss may co-occur with pathways that alter STAT3 dependency, or it may reflect a broader PTEN/PI3K/mTOR/STAT signaling axis. These findings remain associations measured in the supplied lung-cell-line-like dataset; they do not establish causality, drug sensitivity, or external clinical generalizability. I have registered follow-up tests for PTEN/STAT3 robustness and modification, including interaction by TP53 loss, persistence in high-quality screens, and persistence in KRAS-wild-type and non-KY-library contexts.",
