  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive estimate means stronger dependency, i.e. a more negative CRISPR dependency score in the exposed group relative to comparator. For direction +1 claims, a positive estimate means weaker dependency or a higher, less negative dependency score in the exposed group.  Due assessment H6: TP53-wild-type versus TP53-loss status associated with stronger USP7 dependency received independent validation R50: signed estimate +0.8115 with independent interval +0.7821 to +0.8408 at the validation-adjusted level. This matches discovery R6 +0.8019 and is far beyond the small null band seen in rejected predictor tests. H6 is accepted and closed as a robust within-dataset association.  Due assessment H94: PTEN loss versus PTEN-intact status associated with stronger STAT3 dependency received independent validation R76: signed estimate +0.2919 with independent interval +0.2135 to +0.3704. This is concordant with discovery R75 +0.2674, interval +0.2153 to +0.3195. The validation interval excludes zero even with the stricter independent interval. The effect is smaller than the leading four associations but is still scientifically meaningful on the dependency-score scale: dependency_STAT3 has SD about 0.246, so the effect is roughly 1.2 SD, and the lower confidence bound is roughly 0.9 SD. Thus I accept H94 as a newly supported within-dataset association but keep its investigation active pending robustness and specificity checks.  Due assessment H16: the ARID1A-loss interaction in the SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 dependency comparison received independent validation R77: signed estimate +0.0162 with interval -0.0543 to +0.0868. Discovery R16 was +0.0373 with interval -0.0148 to +0.0894. Both are small and compatible with no meaningful modification, especially relative to the huge main SMARCA4-loss effect around +0.78 to +0.81. I reject H16 and close it. This supports interpreting H7 as a broad SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 association not materially modified by ARID1A loss in this dataset.  Synthesis of supported findings: the strongest within-dataset predictor-outcome associations are now (1) TP53-wild-type status with stronger USP7 dependency, (2) SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, (3) MSI-high status with stronger WRN dependency, including the mutation_burden >= 4 restriction, and (4) KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, meaning higher or less negative NFE2L2 dependency scores than KEAP1-intact cells. These four are independently validated and remain large across libraries, culture, batch_a, high screen quality, mutation-burden contexts, aneuploidy strata for USP7, and several co-alteration restrictions. The new fifth supported association is PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency, discovery and validation estimates near +0.27 to +0.29. This is smaller than the leading four but well above the usual null band and above plausible clinical-scale thresholds if dependency-score units are interpreted directly.  Negative findings are substantial: direct same-gene or pathway-target dependency predictions were broadly null, including KRAS mutation/KRAS, BRAF V600E/BRAF, EGFR amplification/EGFR even among KRAS/BRAF wild-type contexts, ERBB2 amplification/ERBB2, PIK3CA/STAT3, MYC/POLR2A, high MYC/RPL11, TP53 loss/MDM2, MSI-high/DHX9, MSI-high/KIF18A, KRAS/RIT1, ERBB2/TMED10, RB1/KIF18A, MYC/MDM2 among TP53-WT, BRCA2 loss with WRN/DHX9/POLR2A, STK11 loss with KRAS/POLR2A/RPL11, and CDKN2A loss with POLR2A. Interaction tests have not shown meaningful modification of the leading associations by ARID1A loss or TP53-loss status, and ARID1A modification of SMARCA2/SMARCA4-loss is now independently null.  Unresolved uncertainty is mainly interpretive and external. The accepted effects are measured associations within this supplied lung-cell-line-like dataset. They do not prove causality, mechanistic synthetic lethality, drug response, or clinical biomarker behavior. The KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 weaker-dependency direction remains biologically counterintuitive and could reflect dataset-specific structure, co-alteration patterns, scoring behavior, or confounding. USP7/TP53-wild-type and PTEN/STAT3 could also reflect correlated genomic or pathway features. The most informative next analysis is to complete the PTEN/STAT3 robustness battery: interaction by TP53 loss, high-quality screens, KRAS wild-type, non-KY library, batch_a, adherent culture, adenocarcinoma subtype, and MYC-amplification interaction, plus one specificity check using PTEN loss against KRAS dependency. If PTEN/STAT3 persists across these contexts, it should be closed as a fifth robust within-dataset association; if it attenuates in key subsets, the claim should be narrowed to the context in which it remains large. Formal library, batch, and lineage interaction tests for the leading four effects remain secondary because their main effects are already robust in those subset analyses.",
  "research_notes": "Validated within-dataset associations: H6 TP53-WT stronger USP7; H7 SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2; H9 MSI-high stronger WRN; H13 KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2; H94 PTEN-loss stronger STAT3 (validated +0.292, interval +0.213 to +0.370). Leading four have effect sizes roughly +0.78 to +0.82, >2 SD; PTEN/STAT3 is about +1.2 SD and newly validated. H94 remains active pending context tests. Newly proposed PTEN/STAT3 robustness: batch_a, adherent culture, adenocarcinoma, MYC interaction, and specificity PTEN/KRAS. Already active H95-H98 test PTEN/STAT3 TP53 interaction, high-quality screens, KRAS-WT, and non-KY. Negative findings: direct-target and many plausible dependency predictors are null; H16 ARID1A interaction null after validation. Next focus: PTEN/STAT3 robustness and specificity; leading-effect library/batch/lineage interactions are lower priority unless needed."
