  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 main effects, a positive estimate means PTEN-loss cells have stronger STAT3 dependency than PTEN-intact cells, i.e. a more negative dependency score. For direction -1 interactions, a positive estimate means the PTEN-loss effect is larger in the named subgroup than in its complement. The interaction H95, testing whether TP53 loss modifies PTEN/STAT3 dependency, gave R78 estimate -0.0967 with 95% interval -0.2171 to +0.0236 in cells with 54 versus 352 observations in the TP53-loss stratum and 212 versus 1382 in the complement. The signed estimate is opposite the registered positive-interaction direction and the interval includes zero, so I reject the claim of positive modification. The negative point estimate raises the possibility that PTEN loss is associated with a smaller STAT3 effect among TP53-loss cells, but the interaction is too imprecise to conclude either attenuation or no modification. I registered direct PTEN-loss effects within TP53-loss and TP53-intact strata to characterize this context more clearly. The MYC-amplification interaction H102, R85 estimate +0.0308 with interval -0.1011 to +0.1627, is small and includes zero, so I reject meaningful positive modification and close it. The context robustness tests support the overall PTEN/STAT3 association. In high-quality screens H96 gave +0.2394, interval +0.1730 to +0.3059; KRAS-wild-type cells H97 gave +0.2726, interval +0.2129 to +0.3323; non-KY library cells H98 gave +0.3020, interval +0.2409 to +0.3632; batch_a H99 gave +0.2337, interval +0.1252 to +0.3421; adherent culture H100 gave +0.2619, interval +0.2027 to +0.3211; and adenocarcinoma subtype H101 gave +0.2677, interval +0.2058 to +0.3295. These intervals all exclude zero and remain near the overall effect magnitude, although H99 is wider because the PTEN-loss cell has only 63 observations. The specificity tests are null or very small. PTEN loss against KRAS dependency H103 gave -0.0135, interval -0.0345 to +0.0076; against USP7 H113 gave +0.0173, interval -0.0308 to +0.0654; against SMARCA2 H114 gave +0.0180, interval -0.0288 to +0.0647; and against WRN H115 gave +0.0033, interval -0.0314 to +0.0380. These are within the usual null band and are orders of magnitude smaller than the leading USP7, SMARCA2, WRN, and NFE2L2 effects. They do not support PTEN loss as a broad dependency predictor for the other leading outcomes. I reject these specificity alternatives and close them. Taken together with the already validated main PTEN/STAT3 association R75/R76, the positive technical and biological stratum estimates, the absence of meaningful MYC interaction, and the null specificity tests, H94 can be accepted and closed as a robust within-dataset association. The direct TP53-loss stratum question remains worth refining because H95 suggested possible attenuation, but it does not negate the overall PTEN-loss/STAT3-dependency association measured in this dataset. These are associations in the supplied dataset, not causal claims, drug-response claims, or externally generalizable biomarker statements. No new validation request is needed: the main H94 claim has already been independently validated, and additional stratum-specific validation would add little relative to completing direct TP53-context estimates.",
