  "narrative": "The new validation evidence materially clarifies the PTEN-loss/STAT3 context and the ARID1A-loss interaction question, while introducing only limited uncertainty for one technical stratum. H94, the overall PTEN-loss versus PTEN-intact association with stronger STAT3 dependency, is strongly supported. The discovery estimate was +0.2674 (95% interval +0.2153 to +0.3195), and the independent validation estimate is +0.2919 with the stricter validation-adjusted interval +0.2135 to +0.3704. Both are already signed to direction -1, so they mean PTEN-loss cells have a more negative dependency_STAT3 score, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency, than PTEN-intact cells. The effect is substantial relative to the STAT3 dependency distribution (SD approximately 0.246) and remains robust across multiple biological and technical contexts. I therefore reaffirm H94 and close it.\n\nH116, the direct PTEN-loss effect among TP53-loss cells, now has independent validation. The signed validation estimate is +0.4417, interval +0.2647 to +0.6186, after the stricter validation adjustment. This is well above zero and is very large relative to the outcome SD (roughly 1.8 SD). It reconciles the earlier uncertainty from the imprecise TP53-interaction estimate: the PTEN-loss/STAT3 association is not restricted to TP53-intact cells. Although the TP53-loss stratum has smaller exposed cell counts, the validation effect is clearly positive, so I accept and close H116. This also deprioritizes the reversed interaction attenuation claim H129; I leave it unresolved as an interaction estimate but set it to deferred because it is not a priority for further follow-up given the direct TP53-loss stratum validation.\n\nH99, the batch_a stratum-specific PTEN-loss/STAT3 effect, is less conclusive. The discovery estimate was positive, +0.2337 with interval +0.1252 to +0.3421, but the independent validation estimate is +0.1587 with interval -0.0178 to +0.3352 under the stricter validation-adjusted threshold. The point estimate remains in the positive direction, but the interval crosses zero. I therefore do not treat this batch_a stratum as independently confirmed. I change it from accept to unresolved active. This does not weaken the overall PTEN-loss/STAT3 association; rather, it marks the batch_a-specific inference as uncertain, probably driven by limited exposed observations and the stringent validation interval.\n\nH16 tested whether ARID1A loss modifies the SMARCA4-loss association with stronger SMARCA2 dependency. The discovery estimate was small and imprecise (+0.0373; interval -0.0148 to +0.0894), and the independent validation estimate is +0.0162 with interval -0.0543 to +0.0868. There is no evidence of a scientifically meaningful positive interaction. I therefore reaffirm rejection and close H16.\n\nOverall, the evidence now supports a focused set of within-dataset associations: TP53-wild-type status with stronger USP7 dependency; SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high status with stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. The PTEN/STAT3 result is now supported across TP53 contexts, PIK3CA and KRAS contexts, non-KY library, high-quality screens, adherent culture, and adenocarcinoma subtype, with the batch_a stratum remaining the main unresolved technical context. The specificity checks remain negative or very small: PTEN loss is not associated with stronger KRAS, USP7, SMARCA2, WRN, MDM2, or NFE2L2 dependency, and PIK3CA mutation among PTEN-intact cells is null for STAT3. The leading accepted predictors also show null cross-specificity against the other leading outcomes. These are associations in the supplied dataset, not causal statements, drug-response claims, or claims of external generalizability. Next, I would not prioritize further PTEN-loss/STAT3 context work except optionally a batch heterogeneity analysis if capacity remains. The more informative next analyses are the selective untested STAT3 predictor probes already registered, such as TP53-wild-type, SMARCA4-loss, MSI-high, KEAP1-loss, MYC amplification, APC mutation, NF1 loss, and SMAD4 loss, plus the mechanistic CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 test among TP53-intact cells and the research-marker-D/STAT3 broad probe. Strong effects in those probes would justify focused follow-up and validation.",
  "research_notes": "Updated current supported within-dataset associations: TP53-WT stronger USP7; SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2; MSI-high stronger WRN; KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2; PTEN-loss stronger STAT3. Direction convention: for direction -1 main effects/TP53-loss strata, positive signed estimate means exposed has stronger, i.e. more negative, dependency; for direction +1, positive signed estimate means weaker, i.e. less negative, dependency. PTEN/STAT3 is now robust across TP53 contexts: overall H94 discovery +0.2674 and validation +0.2919; TP53-intact H117 +0.2870; TP53-loss H116 discovery +0.1903 and validation +0.4417 interval +0.2647 to +0.6186. This resolves the earlier possible-attenuation concern; H95 remains rejected and H129 is deferred/unresolved rather than prioritized. Batch_a PTEN/STAT3 stratum H99 is now unresolved: discovery +0.2337 but validation +0.1587 interval -0.0178 to +0.3352, positive point but strict interval crosses zero. This is a limited technical uncertainty and does not negate H94. ARID1A interaction H16 validated null +0.0162 interval -0.0543 to +0.0868; close. Specificity evidence remains strong: PTEN loss null/near null for KRAS, USP7, SMARCA2, WRN, MDM2, NFE2L2; PIK3CA mutation null for STAT3 among PTEN-intact cells. Accepted predictors show null cross-specificity for TP53-WT/SMARCA2, TP53-WT/WRN, SMARCA4-loss/USP7, MSI-high/SMARCA2, KEAP1-loss/USP7. Negative broad genomic probes include direct-target associations for KRAS mutation/BRAF, EGFR/ERBB2 amplification, ARID1A loss, MYC/STK11/RB1/BRCA2/CDKN2A checks against many outcomes. Main unresolved uncertainties: biological interpretation, possible proxies for correlated genomic programs, external generalizability, counterintuitive KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 weaker-dependency direction, and batch_a stratum uncertainty. Next priorities: selectively test untested STAT3 predictors H105-H112 (TP53-WT, SMARCA4-loss, MSI-high, KEAP1-loss, MYC amplification, APC, NF1 loss, SMAD4 loss), H127 CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 among TP53-intact, and H128 marker-D/STAT3 as a broad constructed-assay probe. If a new strong STAT3 predictor emerges, follow with stratum robustness, outcome specificity, and validation. Avoid causal, therapeutic-response, or externally generalizable claims."
