  "narrative": "The validated evidence now stabilizes a focused set of within-dataset associations. TP53-wild-type status is associated with stronger USP7 dependency (H6, validated by R50). SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H7, validated by R13). MSI-high status is associated with stronger WRN dependency (H9, validated by R63). KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, represented by the reversed accepted claim H13 (validated by R38). PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency, supported overall by validated H94 (R75/R76) and now in the TP53-loss stratum by validated H116 (R90/R102). The ARID1A-loss interaction claim for SMARCA2 was independently refuted (H16, R77). The only main PTEN/STAT3 technical uncertainty is the small batch_a stratum H99: discovery is positive, validation point is positive, but the stricter validation interval crosses zero; I therefore keep H99 unresolved and close it as a direct stratum claim, leaving H130 as a separate batch-interaction probe if capacity permits.\n\nThe most important new finding is CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells associated with stronger MDM2 dependency. The originally registered weaker-dependency direction H127 is rejected because the signed estimate is robustly negative. The reversed refinement H142 has a signed estimate of +0.1185 with interval +0.079 to +0.158 and cell sizes 320 versus 1274. This is about 0.52 SD of the MDM2 dependency distribution and a substantial fraction of its interquartile range. I accept H142 as a robust dataset-level association, while keeping investigation active because independent validation and context/specificity checks remain the most informative next steps. The generic 10% threshold is not naturally interpretable for negative continuous dependency scores, so I used distribution-based magnitude together with interval precision.\n\nNegative findings remain informative. Many other measured predictors are null for STAT3: TP53-wild-type, SMARCA4 loss, MSI-high, KEAP1 loss, MYC amplification, NF1 loss, SMAD4 loss, and PIK3CA mutation among PTEN-intact cells. PTEN loss is not meaningfully associated with KRAS, USP7, SMARCA2, WRN, MDM2, NFE2L2, TMED10, POLR2A, or RPL11 dependency, supporting outcome specificity rather than a broad dependency-state effect. STK11 loss does not mimic KEAP1 loss for NFE2L2. CDKN2A loss does not show the initially hypothesized weaker MDM2 dependency. These are associations in the supplied lung-lineage dataset, not causal or externally generalizable claims.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is to resolve CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 dependency. H143 tests whether the effect disappears or reverses in TP53-loss cells, H144 tests POLR2A outcome specificity, and H145 tests whether the association remains among TP53-intact/MTAP-intact cells. I additionally registered non-KY library and high-quality-screen strata to test technical confounding, plus an MTAP-loss-only test among CDKN2A-intact TP53-intact cells to check 9p21 co-deletion confounding. If these remain consistent, the next priority is independent validation of H142 and the resulting context-restricted refinements. Lower-priority marker and broad specificity probes were deferred to preserve analysis capacity.",
