  "narrative": "The strongest remaining scientific question is whether the reproducible CDKN2A-loss/stronger-MDM2-dependency association is a stable CDKN2A-loss marker across TP53 status and copy-number contexts, or whether it is partially localized to low 9p21/MTAP co-loss. I therefore propose TP53-loss CDKN2A-MDM2 estimates both outside the low-9p21 stratum and inside the low-9p21 stratum. These refine the accepted but less precise TP53-loss CDKN2A claim. I also add a MTAP-loss control outside the low-9p21 stratum, complementing the low-9p21 MTAP control and the active overall MTAP test. If CDKN2A remains positive outside low 9p21 and the active low-versus-complement interaction is small, the evidence will support a relatively stable CDKN2A-loss association. If CDKN2A is null outside low 9p21 while MTAP or interaction signals appear, the association is better described as low-9p21/co-deletion localized. For the PTEN-STAT3 association, I test whether the effect remains among PIK3CA-mutant cells and whether PIK3CA status formally modifies the PTEN effect, which is a biologically coherent PI3K/PTEN context check. I also test CDKN2A loss against STAT3 dependency as an outcome-specificity control, because the main CDKN2A effect is on MDM2 and null probes help limit broad overinterpretation. All new estimates will be interpreted as already signed to the registered direction; for direction -1, positive signed values correspond to stronger dependency in the exposed group. These are within-dataset associations, not causal or generalizability claims. I closed H171 and H172 because their 9p21 thresholds are essentially non-informative duplicates of overall/TP53-intact CDKN2A-MDM2 comparisons, and closed the low-priority marker-D USP7 probe to allocate analysis slots to the CDKN2A specificity and PTEN/PIK3CA follow-up."
}

