ssociated with stronger MDM2 dependency in the primary H5 test and is not a broad driver of STAT3, DHX9, KIF18A, SMARCA2, or WRN effects. MYC amplification, PIK3CA mutation, PIK3CA status as an effect modifier for PTEN-STAT3, STK11 loss, RB1 loss, BRCA2 loss, APC, SMAD4, NF1, MTAP alone, and several constructed marker assays are either null or unsupported as broad dependency predictors. PTEN loss is also relatively specific: it is not associated with KRAS, USP7, SMARCA2, WRN, MDM2, TMED10, POLR2A, RPL11, or NFE2L2 dependency in the major tests.  \n\nRemaining uncertainty is mostly contextual and precision-related, not evidentiary for the headline effects. The PTEN-STAT3 batch-A stratum remains unresolved after validation because the validation interval crossed zero, though the main and several subgroup estimates were robust. Several screen/library interactions remain deferred or unresolved because of sparse cells. The CDKN2A-MDM2 effect remains an association, not a causal statement, and should not be described as externally generalizable or biologically explained solely by a named pathway without external evidence.  \n\nThe most informative next analysis I registered is a new discovery test of CDKN2A loss versus CDKN2A-intact MDM2 dependency among TP53-intact, MTAP-intact cells across all copy-number contexts. This asks whether the CDKN2A-MDM2 association is a stable marker when MTAP co-loss is excluded without forcing low 9p21. If positive, it further supports MTAP-independent CDKN2A-loss biology; if null, the accepted findings are better interpreted as concentrated in low-copy 9p21/co-deletion contexts. No causal or generalizability claims are made.",
