brary and batch interactions remain deferred or unresolved because of sparse cells. The CDKN2A-MDM2 association outside low 9p21 is difficult to test because CDKN2A-loss cells are sparse outside low-copy 9p21, leaving H183 unresolved; H181 and H187 are supportive but sparse. The largest recent unresolved issue is the unexpected research_signature B interaction for PTEN-STAT3. The original H203 was registered with a positive signed heterogeneity hypothesis but produced a large opposite-signed interaction; the reversed follow-up H205 is strong but was registered after inspection and cannot be externally validated because validation budget is exhausted. I therefore keep the signature-B modification unresolved rather than promoting it to a headline finding. It could represent real context dependence of PTEN-STAT3, an arbitrary constructed-signature artifact, or a multiple-comparison pattern. Clarifying it internally is the most informative next analysis.\n\nNext investigation. I keep H205 and H206 active: reversed signature-B interaction and direct high-signature-B PTEN-STAT3 mean difference. I also registered a new complementary low-signature-B test, PTEN-loss versus PTEN-intact STAT3 dependency within research_signature_b <= 0.5. Together these analyses will determine whether the PTEN-STAT3 association is robust below the signature-B threshold, attenuated or reversed above it, or whether the apparent interaction is unstable. Since no voluntary validation budget remains, these are discovery-stage within-dataset checks. The accepted scientific conclusions remain: selective, strong within-dataset associations of TP53 status with USP7, SMARCA4 loss with SMARCA2, MSI-high with WRN, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2, PTEN loss with STAT3, and CDKN2A loss with MDM2, with the low-9p21 CDKN2A-MDM2 stratum now independently validated.",
