dency baseline but is below a more conservative practical benchmark and its interval is not robustly positive; reject and close. H301 RB1-loss RPL11 dependency -0.0204 [-0.0478, 0.0070] is small, imprecise, and in the unexpected signed direction; reject and close. H302 PTEN-loss DHX9 dependency under high screen quality -0.0259 [-0.0564, 0.0047] is small, opposite-signed, and crosses zero; reject and close. H303 PTEN-loss RIT1 dependency under high screen quality +0.0292 [-0.0015, 0.0599] is borderline small and imprecise; reject as not a meaningful supported specificity. H304 TP53-loss POLR2A dependency under high screen quality +0.0107 [-0.0159, 0.0374] is null; reject and close. A critical caveat is that screen-quality eligibility may act as a collider-like filter because screening performance can depend on cell state and genotype, so nulls in H302-H304 are bounded evidence, not proof of no PTEN or TP53 effects. More generally, many repeated constructed-signature and threshold-stratum tests increase false-discovery and false-negativity risk. The acceptances here are confirmations of already accepted axes or context retention, not new core discoveries. To avoid further threshold fishing, I register only three formal interaction refinements for scientifically informative context questions: whether high hypoxia modifies PTEN-loss STAT3, whether MYC RNA level modifies CDKN2A-loss MDM2, and whether STK11 loss modifies SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2.",
